most beacons exposed grch37 data  it's also kind of the most common one in  terms of the data that I'm dealing with  on a day to day basis even though it's  not the latest one so this is actually  not a very surprising statistics we find  something a bit more surprising when we  take a look at the second parameter  which is a chromosome so in the chart  you see how many queries proportionally  targeted a given chromosome and you can  see that out of basically 25 options the  four most feared chromosomes cover more  than 1/2 of all the queries which is  statistically significant and if I have  to take a guess  before doing this analysis what the  distribution would be like I would  probably expect chromosome number 1 to  be in the first place  because it's the largest human  chromosome it's almost 8 percent of our  DNA it's very chin dance over 3,000  genes associated with over 350 diseases  so you would expect it to be up there so  it's it's kind of I mean it's definitely  up there it's in the top 3 but it's kind  of interesting to see that it's not in  the first place chromosome 2 which  claimed the first spot actually also  kind of makes sense because it's the  second largest chromosome again almost 8  percent of our DNA very chin dance  over 1,000 genes so it might be  interesting that it's number one but you  would definitely expect it to be up  there what's really surprising to me is  seeing chromosome 17 in the second spot  because 17 is a pretty short one it's  about freetime shorter than the largest  chromosomes it's not very gene dance so  it's actually very interesting that this  is so heavily studied  so let's take it a bit further and let's  take a look at the searches as a whole  and what kind of variants they target so  in total all our searches covered 85,000  variants distinct variants which might  seem like a lot but if you think about  how much variation that is that's  actually not that much it's actually  pretty focused search so again people  study similar things and in fact if you  take a look at the chart the top 10  variants cover over a quarter of all the  searches which is definitely very  significant and some of them even fall  into the same gene so that's interesting  so now that we have the variance we can  take a look at some of their properties  and one interesting property to look  into would be harmfulness basically you  want to see how many of these variants  are known to be involved in diseases and  obviously what you want to see is a high  number of damaging variants because that  kind of validates the system as a  discovery engine it suggests that people  are looking for stuff that they probably  have seen in their own data set and they  want to see who else has seen this  particular damaging mutation so how do  you determine if a variant is damaging  well you can do it experimentally which  is very accurate but doesn't scale this  mount or you can do it algorithmically  there are prediction algorithms  available that basically rank variants  in terms of their potential for being  harmful and they're not completely  accurate but it's a reasonable  approximation they typically look at  different things as as the basis for  their ranking so it's actually pretty  good advice to run multiple algorithms  you were trying to classify something  and that's what I've done in this case  so I looked at two really common sort of  classic benchmarking algorithms sift and  polyphen - and I ran them across all our  searches there's actually two versions  of polyphen - there's HD of an H bar  which are suitable for different types  of datasets but because beacon network  is a mixture of all the data size I  actually ran both and they got pretty  similar results so I'm only listing one  of them here but the interesting thing  is that safe things 69% of our variants  are damaging and polyphen things that  55% of our variants are damaging which  is quite a high number even if this was  like 10 percent I would say that's  pretty high so 55 and 69 is definitely a  very positive result  so whatever properties of variance can  we study well one thing that was an  important consideration for us when we  were designing that the system was it's  used for rare diseases we've come in  variants quite often you can find  additional information in various public  databases but with rare variants finding  that extra mesh that's really critical  because too many discoveries you need to  have some data to compare against so an  interesting metric for us would be how  many of these variants are actually rare  so how do you determine that the variant  is rare well one way to do this is to  basically take the very end and take a  look at its allele frequency in some  really well-known large data set and  that's what we've done here so we took  the thousand genomes dataset which is a  very famous public sort of reference  dataset for genomic studies and we  cross-referenced all our variants and  compute the allele frequencies in this  dataset a rare variant is typically  defined as a variant that has a little  frequency less than 5% so in our case it  actually turns out that 25% of variants  could be described as rare which again  is quite a lot normally you would have  let's say 1 to 4 percent of rare  variants in an genome so 25% it's quite  high it's a very interesting result so  ok we took a look at variants how can we  push this even further well one thing to  look into would be cross-referencing  variants with genes genes prescribe how  proteins are made so they determine  traits so that's a very interesting  thing to look into  so what done exactly that we've  cross-referenced our data set with the  gene database and we try to count the  number of genes that we've hit so in  total our searches hit almost 10,000  genes which is actually a lot but if you  take a look at the chart top 10 genes  cover almost 50% of all the queries so  again there's a small number of things  that are very heavily studied and I  don't actually have a lot to say about  these genes I can recognize many of them  but you can see that they're associated  with things like breast cancer  pancreatic cancer liver disease that  sort of thing so we can take this even  further right we have variants we kind  of extrapolated to two genes now we can  extrapolate from genes to the actual  diseases and  can take a look at what kind of  conditions people study and that's what  we've done here so we took a look at two  sources of data there's a meme which is  basically a catalog of gene to disease  associations one one problem with Oh  meme is that it doesn't use a controlled  vocabulary so we were doing some sort of  programmatic processing it's hard to  deal with things like synonyms which is  which is why people are increasingly  turning towards oncology's and one  really famous ontology is HBO so HBO  doesn't deal directly with diseases it  deals with clinical abnormalities which  means that you can have things that are  associated with diseases but that's  interesting as well so in terms of  diseases in the left column you can see  that people are heavily searching for  cancers there's pancreatic cancer breast  cancer prostate cancer and that's  interesting to know as for HBO rows 1 2  &amp; 7 are not actual conditions they are  known they're basically what's called  modes of inheritance so there are terms  that describe how certain things are  inherited and of course everything is  inherited so we do expect these terms to  have many hits as for the other stuff  you can see that our issues associated  with spine height weight cognitive  defects birthmarks that sort of thing so  it's interesting to know what people  around the world study so that's all I  had for you today everything that I  talked about is open source and on  github it's easy to find just search for  ga4gh like I said it's a nonprofit  organization largely driven by  volunteering work so if you think that  this is something you would like to be  involved in definitely feel free to  reach out to me and I can get you  involved in this project or point you to  right people who are involved in other  projects and there's tons of exciting  stuff going on within ga4gh if you have  any questions I'll be around for a while  so just come up and say hi and otherwise  I hope you thought this was interesting  and enjoyed the last few hours of  strange loop  you
 

